{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Two more outbound-parent surfaces drop a hierarchy edit with no durable record\n\nFound by the Step 7 parity census while fixing [rebar:9f26-66c2-14af-4223], which closed the same\ngap on the UPDATE emit path. Filed rather than fixed in place because both slots are outside 9f26's\ndeclared `file_impact` and reach into the Cloud/ACLI adapters — a separate blast radius that\ndeserves its own attestation.\n\n## The rule, stated without naming the site 9f26 fixed\n\n**An outbound parent that cannot reach the tracker must leave a durable, operator-visible record\n(a `bridge_alerts` entry), not merely a log line.** This is the project's own contract, cited at\n`pass_io.py:113-118` (\"a log line is not a signal: the pass still exits 0 and nothing durable says\nthe hierarchy diverged\") and `dispatch_one.py:561-564` (\"Warn-and-continue alone is why 'the parent\nnever reached the tracker' stayed invisible through five instances of this class\"). Ticket 39c1\nwired it into ONE of the four surfaces that can drop a parent.\n\n## Channel census — four surfaces, two still MISSING\n\n| # | surface | rule holds? |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `outbound_field_diff._resolve_local_parent` (UPDATE emit) | **HOLDS** — closed by 9f26; records `(jira_key, \"parent\")` on `dropped_field_sink`, which `run_differs._emit_outbound_field_alerts:98` turns into a deduped `outbound-field-dropped` alert |\n| 2 | `adapters/jira/outbound_fields._map_local_to_jira_fields:145-156` (CREATE emit) | **MISSING** — the same unconditional non-epic guard, but its only signal is `logger.debug`, which no operator sees and no test asserts |\n| 3 | `dispatch_one._update_one_apply_parent:523-601` (UPDATE apply) | HOLDS — all three alert kinds |\n| 4 | `adapters/jira/acli_cli_ops._attach_parent_guarded:136-143` (create-then-attach fallback) | **MISSING** — swallows HTTP 400 with a WARNING and returns; no `record_parent_divergence` |\n\nSlot 2 matters because a ticket CREATED with a non-epic parent loses the hierarchy at birth, which\nis harder to notice than losing it on an edit. Slot 4 matters because it is the only path where\nJira actively REJECTED the parent and rebar still reports the create as successful.\n\n## A second finding worth its own decision\n\nThe 8b25 hierarchy guard is **unconditional** (`outbound_field_diff.py:138-139` and\n`adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py:145-147`), and `outbound_field_diff.py:122-123` advertises \"no\nvendor dependency\" — but its stated rationale is vendor-specific: `dispatch_one.py:554-556` scopes\nit to \"**this next-gen project**\", where Jira 400s a non-epic parent. On Data Center a sub-task's\nparent is legitimately a standard issue and DC does **not** 400 it — it accept-and-ignores\n(capability map req-0056/0058). `docs/jira-sync-setup.md:276-291` restates the rule as a universal\n\"standard project\" constraint and never mentions Data Center. So the guard may be suppressing a\nshape whose justification does not hold on DC. 9f26 deliberately did NOT lift it — suppressing is\nstill the right call for DC (the write would be silently ignored) — but the doc/code divergence\nshould be resolved rather than left for the next reader to rediscover.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] slot 2 records a durable bridge alert (not `logger.debug`) when a CREATE drops a non-epic\n      parent, reusing the existing `dropped_field_sink` -> `outbound-field-dropped` channel\n- [ ] slot 4 calls `record_parent_divergence` on its 400-skip, under the existing\n      `outbound-parent-rejected` kind\n- [ ] each is proven RED first: a test that fails for the missing signal before the change\n- [ ] the bound/unbound distinction 9f26 established is preserved — an UNBOUND parent must stay\n      silent, because it converges on a later pass and alerting on it is churn\n- [ ] `docs/jira-sync-setup.md:276-291` states whether the epic-only parent rule is Cloud-specific,\n      and the guard's comments agree with the doc\n---\n\n# Fix plan (rebar-debug Phase 1 exit, 2026-08-05)\n\nBoth silences were re-proven at runtime with positive controls before any code was written — see the\nPhase 1 comment on this ticket. The repair reuses 9f26's mechanism verbatim; it does NOT invent a\nsecond one.\n\n## Slot 2 — CREATE emit: reporter reports, caller judges\n\n9f26's split is the pattern: `_resolve_local_parent` gained a `suppressed_out` list and reports the\nkey it WOULD have emitted, judging nothing, because only the caller can see the remote snapshot; the\ncaller (`diff_canonical_fields`) owns the convergence comparison. Slot 2 gets the identical shape:\n\n1. `adapters/jira/outbound_fields._map_local_to_jira_fields` gains a keyword-only\n   `suppressed_out: list[str] | None = None`. On the 8b25 non-epic branch it appends\n   `binding_store.get_jira_key(local_parent_id)` — **only when that key is truthy**, so an UNBOUND\n   parent stays silent (it converges on a later pass; alerting on it is churn). The returned dict is\n   byte-identical, so nothing rebar sends to Jira changes.\n2. `_backend.OutboundMapper.map_local_to_remote` gains the same optional keyword; Cloud's\n   `_JiraOutbound` forwards it; DC's `_DCOutbound` accepts and ignores it (DC's\n   `_map_local_to_dc_fields` never maps a parent at all, so DC has no suppression to report and its\n   behaviour is unchanged).\n3. `outbound_differ._compute_outbound_create_mutation` receives `dropped_field_sink`, passes a fresh\n   list, and appends `(local_id, \"parent\")` when it is non-empty — the EXISTING channel\n   `run_differs._emit_outbound_field_alerts:101` already turns into a deduped\n   `outbound-field-dropped` bridge alert. The identifier is the LOCAL id because a create has no\n   Jira key yet by construction.\n\n**Why no convergence gate here, and why the tests still vary that axis.** At CREATE the issue does\nnot exist on the tracker, so there is no remote parent to already hold — the drop is unconditionally\na real loss. The converged-vs-divergent axis is therefore protected on the UPDATE side instead: the\nUPDATE caller (`_diff_fields`) passes NO `suppressed_out`, so it cannot alert, and a test pins that a\nbound non-epic parent the tracker ALREADY carries produces no `parent` drop. That is the exact defect\na sibling change shipped past two green gates because its fixture hardcoded `remote_parent_id: None`.\n\n## Slot 4 — ACLI attach fallback\n\n`acli_cli_ops._attach_parent_guarded`'s 400 arm calls\n`pass_io.record_parent_divergence(\"outbound-parent-rejected\", child_key, None, parent_key, exc)`\nalongside the existing WARNING — the same kind and the same helper `dispatch_one.py:571-580` uses for\nthe identical shape. `local_id` is `None` because the transport boundary does not carry it. The\nhelper is fail-open by construction (it swallows every exception), so observability cannot cost a\ncreate that would otherwise land. The 400 is still swallowed and the create still returns — the only\ndelta is a local jsonl append.\n\n## The doc/code divergence (AC5)\n\n`docs/jira-sync-setup.md` states the epic-only parent rule as a universal \"standard project\"\nconstraint and never mentions Data Center. The rule as IMPLEMENTED is unconditional and stays that\nway — 9f26 deliberately did not lift it, because on DC the write would be silently accept-and-ignored\n(capability map req-0056/req-0058), which is worse than suppressing. The doc gains a Data Center\nparagraph saying exactly that: same outcome, different reason (Cloud 400s it; DC accepts and ignores\nit), so the guard is correct on both and is deliberately not vendor-conditional. `dispatch_one.py`'s\n\"this next-gen project\" phrasing is corrected to agree.\n\n## Acceptance / verification\n\n- RED first for each slot, proven failing for the missing signal before any fix exists.\n- Mutation checks that vary converged-vs-divergent, and bound-vs-unbound.\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and CI's own selection\n  `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 4 --dist worksteal -q`.\n- No Cloud-observable behaviour change: no outbound payload, no request, and no return value on\n  either surface differs.\n\n## The exact call sites the `suppressed_out` thread touches (worked example)\n\nThe keyword is added to a Protocol, so every implementation and every call site is enumerated here\nrather than left for the implementer to discover:\n\n1. **The CREATE arm must forward the sink.** `outbound_differ._compute_outbound_mutations` already\n   holds `dropped_field_sink` in scope (`outbound_differ.py:439`) and passes it to the UPDATE arm\n   (`outbound_differ.py:541`, `dropped_field_sink=dropped_field_sink`) — but the CREATE call at\n   `outbound_differ.py:511` does NOT, and `_compute_outbound_create_mutation`'s signature\n   (`outbound_differ.py:597-599`) has no such parameter. Both must change, mirroring the UPDATE arm;\n   without it the appended `(local_id, \"parent\")` never reaches `_emit_outbound_field_alerts`.\n2. **Three test doubles implement `map_local_to_remote` with FIXED signatures ending at\n   `emit_detach_clear=False` and no `**kwargs`**, so each raises `TypeError` the moment the differ\n   passes the new keyword. All three are inside CI's own selection and all three must gain\n   `suppressed_out: list[str] | None = None` (accepted and ignored):\n   - `_RecordingOutboundMapper` — `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_backend_neutrality.py:263`\n   - `_FakeOutbound` — `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/backend_support.py:154`\n   - `PassthroughOutbound` — `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_canonical_outbound_equivalence.py:151`\n3. The two production implementations are `_JiraOutbound.map_local_to_remote`\n   (`adapters/jira/backend.py:63`, forwards it) and `_DCOutbound.map_local_to_remote`\n   (`adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:86`, accepts and ignores it).\n\nWorked example, end to end. Local parent `P` is a `task` bound to `RBJ-1`; unbound local child `C`\nhas `parent_id=P`. `_compute_outbound_create_mutation` allocates `suppressed: list[str] = []` and\ncalls `outbound_mapper.map_local_to_remote(ticket, binding_store=…, local_ticket_types=…,\nsuppressed_out=suppressed)`. Cloud's `_map_local_to_jira_fields` hits the 8b25 non-epic branch,\nlooks up `binding_store.get_jira_key(\"P\") -> \"RBJ-1\"`, appends `\"RBJ-1\"` to `suppressed`, and returns\nthe same dict it returns today (no `parent` key). The caller sees `suppressed` non-empty and appends\n`(\"C\", \"parent\")` to `dropped_field_sink`, which `run_differs._emit_outbound_field_alerts:101` turns\ninto the deduped alert `outbound-field-dropped:C:parent`. Change `P` to an `epic` and `suppressed`\nstays empty and `fields[\"parent\"] == \"RBJ-1\"`; leave `P` unbound and the non-epic branch appends\nnothing because `get_jira_key` returns `None`.\n\n### Slot 4 import direction — deliberate, and lazy\n\n`record_parent_divergence` is imported LAZILY inside `_attach_parent_guarded` (function scope), not\nat module scope, matching the module's existing in-function `import logging as _logging`. Recording\nat the adapter boundary is the correct site, not a layering shortcut: this 400 is raised by\n`client.set_parent` INSIDE the ACLI create-then-attach sequence and is swallowed there, so no core\ncall site ever observes it — `create_issue` returns the created issue and reports success. `pass_io`\nis vendor-neutral (stdlib-only imports; no `adapters` dependency), so the edge introduces no cycle."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785896770749626001,"uuid":"8e42a837-98a8-40eb-92a6-9441ac45312a"}